(192, 6, 'Level 14', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Two Paths', 'The road splits in two. The if-else statement runs one block when the condition is true and a different block when it is false. The guidelines say to always place the statements of an if or if-else block inside brackets { } to avoid confusion.

Declare an int named grade set to 68. If grade is greater than 60, print Congratulations!, otherwise print Sorry you failed.', 'Put else after the closing brace of the if block.', 'int grade = 68; if (grade > 60) { System.out.println("Congratulations!"); } else { System.out.println("Sorry you failed"); }', 60, 8, 14, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'int\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+68;\\s+if\\s*\\(\\s*grade\\s+>\\s+60\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Congratulations!"\\s*\\);\\s*\\}\\s*else\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Sorry\\s+you\\s+failed"\\s*\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart2.png', 'The if-else Statement', 'The road splits in two. The if-else statement runs one block when the condition is true and a different block when it is false. The guidelines say to always place the statements of an if or if-else block inside brackets { } to avoid confusion.

Declare an int named grade set to 68. If grade is greater than 60, print Congratulations!, otherwise print Sorry you failed.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int grade = 68; if (grade > 60) { System.out.println("Congratulations!"); } else { System.out.println("Sorry you failed"); }', '[{"part": "if (grade > 60) { ... }", "explanation": "Runs when the condition is true."}, {"part": "else", "explanation": "Introduces the block that runs when the condition is false."}, {"part": "{ System.out.println(\\"Sorry you failed\\"); }", "explanation": "Printed when grade is 60 or less."}]'),
(193, 6, 'Level 15', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Double Equals', 'A guard checks whether a number is zero, and a common error waits here. Using = instead of == for comparison is wrong: a single = assigns a value, while == compares two values. Also, the condition itself must evaluate to a boolean, so writing if( number ) with an int does not work.

Declare an int named number set to 0. If number is equal to 0, print Zero.', 'Two equals signs: ==', 'int number = 0; if (number == 0) { System.out.println("Zero"); }', 60, 8, 15, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution: int number = 0; if (number == 0) { System.out.println("Zero"); }', 'int\\s+number\\s+=\\s+0;\\s+if\\s*\\(\\s*number\\s+==\\s+0\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Zero"\\s*\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart3.png', 'Comparing With ==', 'A guard checks whether a number is zero, and a common error waits here. Using = instead of == for comparison is wrong: a single = assigns a value, while == compares two values. Also, the condition itself must evaluate to a boolean, so writing if( number ) with an int does not work.

Declare an int named number set to 0. If number is equal to 0, print Zero.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int number = 0; if (number == 0) { System.out.println("Zero"); }', '[{"part": "int number = 0;", "explanation": "The value that will be tested."}, {"part": "number == 0", "explanation": "The == operator compares the two values and gives a boolean."}, {"part": "{ System.out.println(\\"Zero\\"); }", "explanation": "Runs only when the comparison is true."}]'),
(194, 6, 'Level 16', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Cascade', 'The path now branches many times. The else-clause of an if-else can hold another if-else, which lets us cascade conditions to make more complex selections. You can have many else if blocks, and the final else is optional. As soon as one condition is true, the program runs that block and skips the rest. Writing elseif as one word is a common mistake, so keep the two words apart.

Declare an int named grade set to 68. If grade is above 90 print Very good!, else if it is above 60 print Good job!, otherwise print Sorry you failed.', 'if (...) { } else if (...) { } else { }', 'int grade = 68; if (grade > 90) { System.out.println("Very good!"); } else if (grade > 60) { System.out.println("Good job!"); } else { System.out.println("Sorry you failed"); }', 60, 8, 16, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'int\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+68;\\s+if\\s*\\(grade\\s+>\\s+90\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\("Very\\s+good!"\\);\\s*\\}\\s*else\\s+if\\s*\\(grade\\s+>\\s+60\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\("Good\\s+job!"\\);\\s*\\}\\s*else\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\("Sorry\\s+you\\s+failed"\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart4.png', 'The if-else-if Statement', 'The path now branches many times. The else-clause of an if-else can hold another if-else, which lets us cascade conditions to make more complex selections. You can have many else if blocks, and the final else is optional. As soon as one condition is true, the program runs that block and skips the rest. Writing elseif as one word is a common mistake, so keep the two words apart.

Declare an int named grade set to 68. If grade is above 90 print Very good!, else if it is above 60 print Good job!, otherwise print Sorry you failed.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int grade = 68; if (grade > 90) { System.out.println("Very good!"); } else if (grade > 60) { System.out.println("Good job!"); } else { System.out.println("Sorry you failed"); }', '[{"part": "if (grade > 90) { ... }", "explanation": "Checked first. It is false for 68, so Java moves on."}, {"part": "else if (grade > 60) { ... }", "explanation": "Checked next. It is true for 68, so Good job! is printed and the rest is skipped."}, {"part": "else { ... }", "explanation": "Runs only if every earlier condition was false."}]'),
(195, 6, 'Level 17', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Range Gate', 'A gate only opens for a score inside a range. An if statement can make decisions based on ranges of values or conditions. To test that a value is between two limits, join two conditions with the && operator, and put each condition in its own parentheses. Both must be true for the block to run.

Declare a double named grade set to 85.0. If grade is at least 90 print Excellent!, else if grade is below 90 and at least 80 print Good job!', 'else if ((grade < 90) && (grade >= 80))', 'double grade = 85.0; if (grade >= 90) { System.out.println("Excellent!"); } else if ((grade < 90) && (grade >= 80)) { System.out.println("Good job!"); }', 60, 8, 17, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'double\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+85\\.0;\\s+if\\s*\\(grade\\s+>=\\s+90\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\("Excellent!"\\);\\s*\\}\\s*else\\s+if\\s*\\(\\(grade\\s+<\\s+90\\)\\s*&&\\s*\\(grade\\s+>=\\s+80\\)\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\("Good\\s+job!"\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart1.png', 'Conditions With &&', 'A gate only opens for a score inside a range. An if statement can make decisions based on ranges of values or conditions. To test that a value is between two limits, join two conditions with the && operator, and put each condition in its own parentheses. Both must be true for the block to run.

Declare a double named grade set to 85.0. If grade is at least 90 print Excellent!, else if grade is below 90 and at least 80 print Good job!', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'double grade = 85.0; if (grade >= 90) { System.out.println("Excellent!"); } else if ((grade < 90) && (grade >= 80)) { System.out.println("Good job!"); }', '[{"part": "double grade = 85.0;", "explanation": "The value that will be tested."}, {"part": "if (grade >= 90) { ... }", "explanation": "False for 85.0, so Java checks the next condition."}, {"part": "else if ((grade < 90) && (grade >= 80))", "explanation": "Both parts are true for 85.0, so the block runs. && needs both sides true."}, {"part": "{ System.out.println(\\"Good job!\\"); }", "explanation": "Printed because the range check passed."}]'),
(196, 6, 'Level 18', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Switchboard', 'Pip finds a switchboard with a lever for each value. The switch statement is another way to branch on multiple outcomes. The switch expression is an integer or character expression, and each case selector is a unique integer or character constant. Java jumps to the matching case and runs statements from there until it meets a break, so every case should end with break. Unlike an if block, the statements of a case do not need curly braces.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with one case 100 that prints Excellent! and then breaks.', 'switch (grade) { case 100: statement; break; }', 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; }', 60, 8, 18, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution: int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; }', 'int\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+100;\\s+switch\\s*\\(\\s*grade\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*case\\s+100:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Excellent!"\\s*\\);\\s+break;\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart2.png', 'The switch Statement', 'Pip finds a switchboard with a lever for each value. The switch statement is another way to branch on multiple outcomes. The switch expression is an integer or character expression, and each case selector is a unique integer or character constant. Java jumps to the matching case and runs statements from there until it meets a break, so every case should end with break. Unlike an if block, the statements of a case do not need curly braces.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with one case 100 that prints Excellent! and then breaks.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; }', '[{"part": "switch (grade)", "explanation": "Evaluates grade and jumps to the matching case."}, {"part": "case 100:", "explanation": "The case selector. The colon starts the statements for this case."}, {"part": "System.out.println(\\"Excellent!\\");", "explanation": "Runs because grade is 100."}, {"part": "break;", "explanation": "Stops Java from running the statements of the cases after this one."}]'),
(197, 6, 'Level 19', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Default Door', 'What if no lever matches? If none of the cases are satisfied, the default block is executed. The default part is optional, and a switch can have no default block at all. It is written with the word default followed by a colon, and it usually goes last.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with case 100 printing Excellent! and breaking, and a default that prints Sorry, you failed.', 'default: comes after the last case.', 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; default: System.out.println("Sorry, you failed."); }', 60, 8, 19, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'int\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+100;\\s+switch\\s*\\(\\s*grade\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*case\\s+100:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Excellent!"\\s*\\);\\s+break;\\s+default:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*"Sorry,\\s*you\\s+failed\\."\\s*\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart3.png', 'switch With default', 'What if no lever matches? If none of the cases are satisfied, the default block is executed. The default part is optional, and a switch can have no default block at all. It is written with the word default followed by a colon, and it usually goes last.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with case 100 printing Excellent! and breaking, and a default that prints Sorry, you failed.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; default: System.out.println("Sorry, you failed."); }', '[{"part": "case 100: ... break;", "explanation": "Runs when grade is 100."}, {"part": "default:", "explanation": "The fallback block. It runs when no case selector matches."}, {"part": "System.out.println(\\"Sorry, you failed.\\");", "explanation": "Printed for any grade that is not 100."}]'),
(198, 6, 'Level 20', 'STRINGS AND DECISIONS', 'The Grand Crossroads', 'At the Grand Crossroads every road meets. When a case is matched, Java runs its statements and also the statements of the cases that follow, unless a break stops it. That is why each case ends with break. Deciding between an if statement and a switch statement is a judgment call: a switch decides on a single integer or character value, and each case value must be unique.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with case 100 printing Excellent!, case 90 printing Good job!, case 80 printing Study harder!, each followed by break, and a default printing Sorry, you failed.', 'Three cases, each ending in break, then a default.', 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; case 90: System.out.println("Good job!"); break; case 80: System.out.println("Study harder!"); break; default: System.out.println("Sorry, you failed."); }', 100, 8, 20, 1, '2026-10-10 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'int\\s+grade\\s+=\\s+100;\\s+switch\\s*\\(grade\\)\\s*\\{\\s*case\\s+100:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\("Excellent!"\\);\\s+break;\\s+case\\s+90:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\("Good\\s+job!"\\);\\s+break;\\s+case\\s+80:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\("Study\\s+harder!"\\);\\s+break;\\s+default:\\s+System\\.out\\.println\\("Sorry,\\s*you\\s+failed\\."\\);\\s*\\}', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart4.png', 'A Full switch Statement', 'At the Grand Crossroads every road meets. When a case is matched, Java runs its statements and also the statements of the cases that follow, unless a break stops it. That is why each case ends with break. Deciding between an if statement and a switch statement is a judgment call: a switch decides on a single integer or character value, and each case value must be unique.

Declare an int named grade set to 100. Write a switch on grade with case 100 printing Excellent!, case 90 printing Good job!, case 80 printing Study harder!, each followed by break, and a default printing Sorry, you failed.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int grade = 100; switch (grade) { case 100: System.out.println("Excellent!"); break; case 90: System.out.println("Good job!"); break; case 80: System.out.println("Study harder!"); break; default: System.out.println("Sorry, you failed."); }', '[{"part": "switch (grade)", "explanation": "Picks the case that matches the value of grade."}, {"part": "case 100: ... break;", "explanation": "Prints Excellent! and stops."}, {"part": "case 90: ... break;", "explanation": "Prints Good job! and stops."}, {"part": "case 80: ... break;", "explanation": "Prints Study harder! and stops."}, {"part": "default: ...", "explanation": "Runs when none of the cases match."}]');

